<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Project-level Incredibuild Linux profile: CACHE ONLY, NO DISTRIBUTION.

  This file is ib_profile.xml with ONE attribute changed:

      ib_profile.xml             <process filename="rustc" type="allow_remote">
      ib_profile.cache-only.xml  <process filename="rustc" type="local_only">

  Everything else (the globals block, the process list, the ib_cache child and
  its enabled="true") is identical, and scripts/robot-demo/ib-benchmark.sh
  CHECKS that mechanically before it measures anything, so the two files cannot
  drift into differing in some second, unnoticed way.

  Why the one word matters. /opt/incredibuild/data/ib_profile.xsd declares
  `type` and `ib_cache` as independent things on the same <process>:

      <xs:simpleType name="type_type">
        <xs:restriction base="xs:string">
          <xs:enumeration value="intercepted" />
          <xs:enumeration value="static_intercepted" />
          <xs:enumeration value="static_intercepted_fileops" />
          <xs:enumeration value="allow_remote" />
          <xs:enumeration value="local_only" />
        </xs:restriction>
      </xs:simpleType>

  and ib_cache is a separate child element of process_type carrying its own
  required `enabled` attribute. Remote execution and Build Cache reuse are
  therefore two knobs, not one. type="local_only" keeps rustc INTERCEPTED and
  CACHED while guaranteeing that no rustc task is ever sent to a helper.

  That is what makes a cache measurement possible at all. With allow_remote, a
  faster wall time could be the cache, the helpers, or both, and nothing in the
  wall time says which. With local_only, Incredibuild's own Build History
  reports remote_tasks=0 and remote_core_time=0 for the build, and `swf-cli
  robot-demo build-proof` run with `distribution excluded` REFUSES the receipt
  if either is anything else. Whatever acceleration is left is the cache's.

  Incredibuild loads $PWD/ib_profile.xml with highest precedence, so this file
  is not read where it sits. ib-benchmark.sh COPIES the selected profile over
  rust/ib_profile.xml inside the disposable git worktree it builds in, records
  the SHA-256 of the file it installed, and never edits the checked-out tree.
-->
<ib_profile version="1">
  <globals
    ignore_following_profiles="false"
    dummy_mode="false"
    monitor_enable="false"
    local_slot_priority="low"
    requested_cores="10000"
  />
  <processes>
    <process filename="rustc" type="local_only">
      <ib_cache enabled="true" />
    </process>
  </processes>
</ib_profile>
